WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.110 --> 00:00:07.700
Speaker 1: And speaking of Democrats pushing
much needed legislation this week, several

00:00:07.700 --> 00:00:16.800
Democratic members in the House of Representatives
and in the Senate have proposed excuse me,

00:00:16.800 --> 00:00:25.180
legislation called the Right to Vote Act and
what the Right to Vote Act, which let's give

00:00:25.180 --> 00:00:26.210
credit here.

00:00:26.210 --> 00:00:31.580
You got Representative Mondher Jones, Senators
John Office and Alex Padilla.

00:00:31.580 --> 00:00:34.320
They introduced this piece of legislation
on Wednesday.

00:00:34.320 --> 00:00:41.570
What it would do is would ensure that courts
respect the right to vote as the fundamental

00:00:41.570 --> 00:00:43.480
right that it is.

00:00:43.480 --> 00:00:48.030
It is the first congressional guarantee of
the right to vote in federal elections.

00:00:48.030 --> 00:00:52.720
It also solves the problem that we are seeing
in certain states of elections, subversion.

00:00:52.720 --> 00:00:56.610
The bill would affirm American's right to
vote by declaring election laws or rules that

00:00:56.610 --> 00:01:01.739
pose a severe or discriminatory burden to
be illegitimate unless the jurisdiction in

00:01:01.739 --> 00:01:07.850
question can prove that that its proposed
law or rule furthers a governmental interest

00:01:07.850 --> 00:01:12.960
and is the least restrictive means of doing
so.

00:01:12.960 --> 00:01:19.560
So once again, we have Democrats in the House
and in the Senate.

00:01:19.560 --> 00:01:25.080
That are saying we have to do something to
protect the right to vote for Americans.

00:01:25.080 --> 00:01:29.820
You know, what is it, close to 200 pieces
of legislation being worked on across the

00:01:29.820 --> 00:01:30.820
country.

00:01:30.820 --> 00:01:35.200
We all know Texas right now is trying to be
the latest to pass a massively restrictive

00:01:35.200 --> 00:01:36.460
voting bill.

00:01:36.460 --> 00:01:37.770
George has already done it.

00:01:37.770 --> 00:01:39.659
Florida's already done it.

00:01:39.659 --> 00:01:43.280
Other states are trying to get it done as
well.

00:01:43.280 --> 00:01:48.390
Federal legislation could circumvent this
federal legislation, could render all of these

00:01:48.390 --> 00:01:52.500
other pieces dead.

00:01:52.500 --> 00:01:56.290
Here's the problem.

00:01:56.290 --> 00:02:02.010
In some of the areas where this legislation
has passed, and I'm not saying this would

00:02:02.010 --> 00:02:05.619
be effective, I'm just saying this is a possibility.

00:02:05.619 --> 00:02:11.410
You have states that have already passed these
restrictive voting bills.

00:02:11.410 --> 00:02:17.300
If the federal government were to come out
with federal legislation basically undoing

00:02:17.300 --> 00:02:22.010
these state bills, the states would actually
have a pretty decent 10th Amendment case to

00:02:22.010 --> 00:02:24.620
bring against the government.

00:02:24.620 --> 00:02:30.930
You know, you didn't have a law in place,
so we put our own in place.

00:02:30.930 --> 00:02:36.290
And so hours existed prior to yours, it may
not be the strongest legal argument, but I

00:02:36.290 --> 00:02:39.450
guarantee you we would see it coming out of
these states.

00:02:39.450 --> 00:02:43.840
You know, the states rights groups would be
fighting this tooth and nail.

00:02:43.840 --> 00:02:45.599
Doesn't mean it would be successful.

00:02:45.599 --> 00:02:51.050
Just means if something like the Right to
Vote Act did end up passing, it would still

00:02:51.050 --> 00:02:57.430
have a long journey through the U.S. courts
before finally becoming law of the land, to

00:02:57.430 --> 00:03:02.099
